Evaluation Operators


1. $regex
--------------------------------------------------------------
What is $regex?

$regex is used to search patterns in strings instead of exact values.

Without $regex:

db.employees.find({
    name: "tayyab"
});

Only returns:

tayyab

It won't return:
tayyab ali
muhammad tayyab
TAYYAB

With $regex, you can search patterns.

Examples–
=>Names starting with "t"
db.employees.find({
    name: {
        $regex: "^t"
    }
    
=>Names ending with "a"
db.employees.find({
    name: {
        $regex: "a$"
    }
});

=>Contains "an"
db.employees.find({
    name: {
        $regex: "an"
    }
});

Returns
tania
imran

=>Case Sensitivity
Suppose the database contains

Tayyab
tayyab
TAYYAB

This query
db.employees.find({
    name: {
        $regex: "^t"
    }
});

returns only
tayyab

because MongoDB regex is case-sensitive by default.

Solution:

Use:
$options: "i"
Example
db.employees.find({
    name: {
        $regex: "^t",
        $options: "i"
    }
});

Now it returns

Tayyab
tayyab
TAYYAB
---------------------------------------------
2. $expr
What is $expr?

Normally MongoDB compares a field with a fixed value.

Example
db.employees.find({
    salary: {
        $gt: 40000
    }
});

But what if you want to compare one field with another field?

Example
salary > bonus
You can't do this with $gt alone.
That's where $expr comes in.

Suppose a document is

{
    name:"Ali",
    salary:50000,
    bonus:30000
}

db.employees.find({
    $expr: {
        $gt: ["$salary","$bonus"]
    }
});

Meaning
salary > bonus

=>Another example

Find employees where

age > salary
db.employees.find({
    $expr:{
        $gt:[
            "$age",
            "$salary"
        ]
    }
});

Probably returns nothing, but the syntax is correct.
Inside $expr you can use
$gt
$gte
$lt
$lte
$eq
$ne
